A||N||3||Seanh||New Rack and Pinion alignment?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||06-16-2001||11:36 AM||||I haven't screwed with my car yet, just drove the other one today. I am getting my new rack and pinion today on my lunch break. Anyway, I read in Haynes that you should scribe a mark on your rack assey before removing it, so it will be realigned with your steering wheel. Well, I'm getting a new rack and pinion, so how do I align it with the steering wheel. There very well may be instructions with it for this, but I haven't gotten it yet, so I would like to make sure I know what to do tonight. Are there any tricks I can use to get the wheel alignment correct? I will realign it anyway, considering we hit a post @ 20mph and the front passenger wheel took the impact, it might be a *tad* bit off. I just want the inital alignment to be good because I might not get it in for a week or so. Thanks!<P>If anyone has any helpful information from experience about removing and replacing the rack and pinion assembly, let me know.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||06-16-2001||12:18 PM||||Before you remove your old rack, turn your steering wheel so the front wheels are straight. Measure both sides from the inner tie rod end to the outer tie rod end where it passes through the steering knuckle. (the grease fitting will do)<BR>Remove rack.<BR>Remove your outer tie rod ends and count the # of revolutions it takes to remove each one. Install the outer ends on the new rack with the same # of revolutions it took to remove them, but do not tighten lock nuts.<BR>Center your steering wheel inside the car, and adjust your new rack with the first measurements and install rack. Remember to tighten everything up after you verify the measurements after installation.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||06-16-2001||02:08 PM||||You don't happen to know of any diagrams on the net that would show the steering assembly in detail do you? Granted I haven't ever messed with the front end and steering system, I am having trouble understanding where the inner tie rod ends and the outter tie rod begins. I'm sure it will be more clear once I'm under the car, but I was just curious while I'm at work. That, and I have nothing better to do since there is no business due to NebraskaLand days celebration crap. Thanks, I now understand what to do, but still unsure about tie rod seperation.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||06-16-2001||03:01 PM||||I can't find any pics, but when you look it will explain itself. The inner tie rod starts at the rubber boot (looks like an accordion) at either end of the rack. Measure from the backside of this to the end where it attaches to the knuckle (where the grease fitting is). The outer tie rod end has a lock nut or jamb nut to lock it in place, this is between the boot and the end. Don't worry, you'll see it. The outer tie rod will unscrew off the rod end once you loosen the nut.||||reg||1
